<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-number-systems">
  <title>Number Systems</title>
  <section xml:id="number-systems-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>Natural numbers count discrete objects; integers also include zero and negative numbers. Rational numbers can be written as a ratio of two integers with a non-zero denominator. Irrational numbers cannot be written in this form. Every rational and irrational number is real.</p></introduction>
    <p>The number line gives a geometric representation of ordered real numbers. The same number may belong to more than one number system.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="number-systems-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-number-systems">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Classify −4, 3/5 and √2.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>−4 is an integer and rational; 3/5 is rational; √2 is irrational. All three are real.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-number-systems">
      <statement><p>Is 0.75 rational? Explain.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Yes. It is equal to 3/4.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
